            "Heat, humidity, crowding, shared towels and combs, barefoot farming, insect bites, scratching, malnutrition, HIV and diabetes all break the barrier or feed what gets through it. Most skin infections seen at a CHPS compound or outpatient department can be diagnosed by eye and treated with a handful of drugs — but three things go wrong repeatedly: the <strong>wrong depth</strong> (creams for a scalp ringworm that needs tablets, antibiotics for an abscess that needs a blade), the <strong>wrong diagnosis</strong> (‘cellulitis’ that is necrotising fasciitis, a ‘fungal patch’ that is leprosy, ‘eczema’ that is scabies) and the <strong>wrong household</strong> (the child is treated and re-infected by siblings, towels and combs the same week). This lesson arranges the bacterial, fungal and mycobacterial infections so that you can name them, treat them at the right depth and break the chain around them; Lesson 4.2 does the same for viruses and parasites.",
